why: one of the four items SPRINT.md names as open for whoever picks
  next, and the only one of them that is a plain measured gap rather
  than a research programme. The entry is one sentence and a number:

    bracket-over-region — `okayBracket` 27.2 against `okayResource`
    22.4 (§7), 21%.

  docs/benchmarks.md §7 publishes the same gap from another session
  (region 22.5, bracket 29) under a heading that reads as if the two
  columns did the same work.

FIRST QUESTION, and it must be asked before any optimisation: IS THIS
A PAIR? This repository has been bitten by exactly that
(benchmark-pairing-rule: five "we lose" rows against ZIO were
mismatched). Read:

  - `okayBracket` acquires, uses and releases N times, releasing
    EAGERLY after each use.
  - `okayResource` acquires N times in ONE region and releases all N
    at the end, in reverse.

  Different semantics — but comparable work: N acquires and N
  releases either way. The region additionally builds a list of N
  finalizers; bracket keeps a depth of one. So the pair is defensible
  and the gap is a real question, not an artefact. What the table
  must not do is imply the two are interchangeable, which its heading
  currently does.

WHERE I EXPECT THE 21% TO BE. `bracket` is:

    pure[F, Unit](()).flatMap: _ =>
      val r = acquire
      try pure(use(r).runWith)
      finally release(r)

  per step: one `pure`, one `flatMap`, one more `pure` — and
  `use(r).runWith`, which is A WHOLE NESTED INTERPRETER RUN inside
  each bracket. The region is one drive over a while-loop with a
  finalizer list, which the doc already says is "visible in the
  number".

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - the nested `runWith` is not an accident, it is the GUARANTEE:
    "the use-program runs to completion inside one suspension, so no
    outer handler can skip or repeat the release". If the 21% is that
    run, then removing it is not an optimisation, it is a weakening,
    and the honest answer is to price it and decline — as
    source-unfold-tuple, drain-copy-per-element, jiffy-hole-scan and
    adaptive-concurrency were all declined this week.
  - one round lies. Per-lane minimum of 3+, and the box has carried
    sibling builds all evening; a control lane must hold across the
    rounds or the numbers are the machine.
  - 21% of 27 microseconds per 1000 steps is 5 nanoseconds a step. It
    has to be worth having before it is worth doing.

predict: MOSTLY THE NESTED RUN, and therefore mostly not removable.
  I expect the two spare `pure` nodes to be a minority share — under
  a third of the gap — and the honest outcome to be a decomposed
  table plus a corrected §7 heading, not a faster bracket. Recorded
  so being wrong shows.
